WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:49.715
[country music]

00:00:51.923 --> 00:00:54.487
[Peter] So Eric,
this is all your family's property?

00:00:54.561 --> 00:00:56.717
-Yep. I'm a sixth generation Waterman,

00:00:56.784 --> 00:01:00.678
and we've owned the same piece of property
since Winslow Waterman came over

00:01:00.745 --> 00:01:05.495
from North Haven and bought it
from one of Henry Knox's war buddies.

00:01:05.923 --> 00:01:08.200
-You guys even have
an island out here, right?

00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:11.047
It's this one right here
with the trees, Eben Island.

00:01:11.114 --> 00:01:12.627
-We're going to get out there?

00:01:12.694 --> 00:01:15.038
-Yep. Better start swimming.
-[Peter laughs]

00:01:15.105 --> 00:01:16.526
That's Tommy Island.

00:01:16.593 --> 00:01:17.773
Just one house on it?

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.915
Yeah. Trip family built a house out there,
and I got a camp over on there.

00:01:21.982 --> 00:01:24.927
-So deep history here.
A lot of entrenched families.

00:01:24.994 --> 00:01:26.119
Been here generations.

00:01:26.186 --> 00:01:30.536
It's changing. Used to be more,
but I think we're the last holdout.

00:01:30.603 --> 00:01:32.536
-You're the last holdout?
-Yep.

00:01:32.619 --> 00:01:35.503
I have three sisters,
and we all own this together.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.820
These old barns, everyone says,
"Oh, it's too bad to let them go."

00:01:38.887 --> 00:01:41.973
But, man,
we're gonna have to tear them down.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.739
-How many acres do you guys have?

00:01:43.806 --> 00:01:46.859
-We only have three and a half acres
of original farmland here.

00:01:46.926 --> 00:01:49.004
-New houses like that? What's that story?

00:01:49.071 --> 00:01:51.431
My grandparents passed away,

00:01:51.498 --> 00:01:55.605
and the family had an opportunity
to buy the house, but nobody bought it.

00:01:55.672 --> 00:01:58.773
Someone from out of state
came and bought it.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:00.353
Probably $4 million.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.489
-$4 million?!
-I'm not kidding. It's unreal.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.933
-So this is totally in transition.
You got the old trailer here.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.830
You've got the new $4 million place.

00:02:07.897 --> 00:02:09.863
We call this the Waterman Beach Ghetto.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.180
We already went through it.
It's not very big.

00:02:12.247 --> 00:02:14.546
-If you blink, you'll miss it.
-All right.

00:02:14.613 --> 00:02:17.689
So is that the story right now
up here in Maine?

00:02:17.756 --> 00:02:21.393
You've got the old local families,
and then second home type stuff?

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:23.278
Yeah. We're dwindling.

00:02:23.345 --> 00:02:26.606
It's just like my daughter.
She's going to school in Rhode Island.

00:02:26.673 --> 00:02:28.598
She's probably not gonna live here.

00:02:28.665 --> 00:02:32.652
My daughter Abby, I'm pretty sure will.
'cause she fishes and has her own boat.

00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:35.269
-How old is your daughter?
-21. You'll see her boat.

00:02:35.336 --> 00:02:38.218
-She's going to go with us.
-We're going to see her today?

00:02:38.285 --> 00:02:41.152
Yeah.
This was all family, distant cousins.

00:02:41.219 --> 00:02:43.298
I had to go three counties over

00:02:43.365 --> 00:02:46.246
to find a girl
I wasn't related to for a girlfriend.

00:02:46.313 --> 00:02:47.146
-Really?

00:02:47.213 --> 00:02:49.946
[laughing] No. Third cousins don't count.

00:02:51.898 --> 00:02:53.498
This is the Gig Village.

00:02:54.141 --> 00:02:56.509
It's the Wessaweskeag River. Indian name.

00:02:56.576 --> 00:02:59.385
And there's George Kittredge's
yellow submarine.

00:02:59.452 --> 00:03:00.769
What's going on with this?

00:03:00.836 --> 00:03:02.786
That was a great friend of my father's.

00:03:02.853 --> 00:03:06.084
He was in World War II.
He was a commander of two submarines.

00:03:06.151 --> 00:03:07.902
He had a submarine school.

00:03:07.969 --> 00:03:12.500
He visioned people being able to
explore underwater by themselves.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:14.623
-That is cool. Look at that.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:18.150
-He had a subtender.
He'd put them on the stern of the boat.

00:03:18.217 --> 00:03:19.299
-All right.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:22.278
That's his railway.
That's where he used to haul his…

00:03:22.345 --> 00:03:23.178
-Sweet.

00:03:23.245 --> 00:03:27.066
-We went out and salvaged
a sunk boat in 120 feet of water

00:03:27.133 --> 00:03:29.387
when I was 13 years old.

00:03:31.275 --> 00:03:33.636
-You want to go into Keeg's store?
-Not really.

00:03:33.703 --> 00:03:34.654
What's in there?

00:03:34.721 --> 00:03:36.848
Just a bunch of f*cking greasy locals.

00:03:36.915 --> 00:03:39.287
-All out-of-staters.
-[Peter laughs]

00:03:39.354 --> 00:03:40.850
We got better things to do.

00:03:40.917 --> 00:03:43.280
-Okay. So right now,
what's the dynamic like?

00:03:43.347 --> 00:03:46.581
Locals versus out-of-staters
or second homes or…

00:03:46.648 --> 00:03:50.395
My dynamic is they say
good fences make good neighbors,

00:03:50.462 --> 00:03:53.423
but I just think good neighbors
make good neighbors.

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.256
-Okay.
-Don't be a douche.

00:03:55.329 --> 00:03:56.203
Right.

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:59.509
Treat people how you're treated.
And if they treat you like crap…

00:03:59.576 --> 00:04:01.243
Double it up. Send it back.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:03.465
But if they come in cool,
you guys are cool.

00:04:03.532 --> 00:04:04.507
Oh, yeah. No, yeah.

00:04:04.574 --> 00:04:07.412
There's no animosity, no anger.
That's what family's for.

00:04:07.479 --> 00:04:08.809
[laughter]

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:13.772
So this is your place?

00:04:13.839 --> 00:04:15.137
-Yeah, this is my house.

00:04:15.204 --> 00:04:16.804
Yeah, my nice messy pad.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.468
-Ah, that's cool. It's a nice place.

00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:21.494
[plays piano]

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.040
I can't read music. I just mess around.

00:04:28.107 --> 00:04:30.592
[continues playing]

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.507
That's about it.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:41.860
There's a picture of… that crashed,
I don't know, 20, 30 miles offshore.

00:04:41.927 --> 00:04:42.760
-Okay.

00:04:42.827 --> 00:04:45.452
-My father got the last known location
from the Coast Guard,

00:04:45.519 --> 00:04:50.153
and he went out in his lobster boat,
and right before dark, he found it.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:53.386
He brought a pilot friend of his
who was a Navy pilot with him

00:04:53.453 --> 00:04:55.677
in case they could
get it going and fly home.

00:04:55.744 --> 00:04:56.577
-Nice.

00:04:56.644 --> 00:04:59.303
Ended up towing it
into Criehavenen Harbor.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:03.223
Put it on a moor and then sold it
back to the insurance company.

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:04.471
Everybody wins, right?

00:05:04.538 --> 00:05:06.218
-What's the story with this wheel?

00:05:06.285 --> 00:05:09.809
Ah, it's just decoration.
It's kind of a fishing house, isn't it?

00:05:09.876 --> 00:05:12.925
-Yeah, but it's a nice one.
I've been in some fishing houses

00:05:12.992 --> 00:05:15.798
where I got a buddy
in Smith Island, Maryland.

00:05:16.383 --> 00:05:19.989
The water comes in his house and
the minnows come in with the big tides.

00:05:20.056 --> 00:05:20.993
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:23.383
So your place is doing pretty well,
I gotta say.

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:26.726
Yeah. There's a Halibut on the wall.
A friend of mine's father made that.

00:05:26.793 --> 00:05:29.393
That was me in high school,
hardhat diving.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.231
When I was like 14.

00:05:31.298 --> 00:05:34.353
And this is my boat.
Bought that boat when I was 19.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.678
So this is funny. I found this picture.

00:05:36.745 --> 00:05:40.129
That's me in the rigging
taking a picture with a throwaway camera.

00:05:40.196 --> 00:05:41.980
And that's the picture I took.

00:05:42.047 --> 00:05:42.880
-Oh, nice.

00:05:42.947 --> 00:05:44.756
We were steaming into the sun.

00:05:44.823 --> 00:05:48.090
It was Asher Alley.
He had a steel scallop dragger.

00:05:48.715 --> 00:05:49.883
We're going to swing down.

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:51.740
Me and Peter are going to stop down

00:05:51.807 --> 00:05:54.786
and you and I
are gonna swap a few stories.

00:05:54.853 --> 00:05:57.263
-Oh, I thought you said
something about storage.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:00.330
No, no.
There's none left upstairs. It's full.

00:06:00.773 --> 00:06:01.926
-Okay.

00:06:01.993 --> 00:06:03.727
-Oh, I'm right here.
-Okay.

00:06:03.794 --> 00:06:07.008
-We'll talk to you later. In a minute.
-Yeah. Yeah, buddy.

00:06:07.075 --> 00:06:08.901
-Who's this guy?
-Buzzy.

00:06:08.968 --> 00:06:10.702
-Buzzy?
-Buzzy Kinney, yeah.

00:06:11.238 --> 00:06:13.273
-Buzzy an old school local?
-Oh, yeah.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:16.256
-You could make a whole show about him.
-About Buzzy?

00:06:16.323 --> 00:06:18.611
He got his girlfriend pregnant.

00:06:18.678 --> 00:06:21.514
He had to quit school to go to work.

00:06:21.581 --> 00:06:24.282
I fished with him
since I was 10 years old.

00:06:24.818 --> 00:06:27.485
[Peter] So you can make a good life
doing that here?

00:06:27.552 --> 00:06:29.275
You can't come from away and do it

00:06:29.342 --> 00:06:31.861
because rules and regulations
are so strict.

00:06:31.928 --> 00:06:34.089
And being able to get licenses to do it.

00:06:34.156 --> 00:06:35.706
And then you've got to be accepted.

00:06:35.773 --> 00:06:38.805
You can't just go and say,
"Hey, I'm going to go lobstering."

00:06:38.872 --> 00:06:40.174
It's very territorial.

00:06:40.676 --> 00:06:43.982
Okay, so there's like some sort of
committee that figures that out?

00:06:44.049 --> 00:06:46.155
No, it's just you fish where you're from.

00:06:46.222 --> 00:06:48.274
Unless you buy into it.

00:06:48.341 --> 00:06:51.702
It's a gentleman's agreement, basically.
Invisible lines.

00:06:51.769 --> 00:06:53.273
-We use landmarks.
-Okay.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:56.353
So you line up granny's house
on the point.

00:06:56.420 --> 00:07:00.011
You know, use like landmarks,
like range marks, but…

00:07:00.078 --> 00:07:02.499
-You guys respect that?
-Yeah, pretty good.

00:07:02.566 --> 00:07:04.053
No one crosses the line?

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:05.907
All friendships end at the line.

00:07:05.974 --> 00:07:09.174
If you don't know where the line is,
keep coming, you'll find it.

00:07:09.241 --> 00:07:10.081
[Peter laughs]

00:07:10.148 --> 00:07:12.029
Pull up alongside them with a

00:07:12.096 --> 00:07:15.313
big old Dexter-Russell knife
on a stone, sharpening it up.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:16.850
-You know?
-[sharpening sound]

00:07:16.917 --> 00:07:18.184
-Okay.
-No, I don't.

00:07:18.755 --> 00:07:20.473
That's what the old timers did.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:21.693
-They fought over it?

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:24.077
-Yeah, it's not worth it. I don't fight.

00:07:24.144 --> 00:07:27.286
-But look at this.
How it comes right into the bay.

00:07:27.353 --> 00:07:30.218
It's very unique.
It's very specific to Maine, right?

00:07:30.285 --> 00:07:32.493
All these rocky outcoves and whatnot.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:36.463
-Mm-hmm. I think it's 3,500 miles
of coastline, I think.

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:40.823
-Yeah, and I forget it, but I think
it's something like 4,000 islands.

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:41.790
Like that many.

00:07:41.857 --> 00:07:43.359
The winds Norwest.

00:07:43.997 --> 00:07:46.357
-Are we looking okay?
-Oh, yeah, we'll be fine.

00:07:46.424 --> 00:07:47.834
We're looking good?

00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:52.058
-This cemetery, the land
was given to the town by my family.

00:07:52.125 --> 00:07:55.452
-A lot of your family members here?
-Yeah. A lot of old names here.

00:07:55.519 --> 00:07:57.986
The Overtons, they came from Finland.

00:07:58.798 --> 00:07:59.893
-Oh, no way.
-Yeah.

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:01.673
-The Finns came here.
-Yeah.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.666
There was a lot of Finns,
Swedes, Norwegian.

00:08:05.185 --> 00:08:08.201
The big thing was
working in the quarries, cutting granite.

00:08:08.268 --> 00:08:09.101
-Oh, right.

00:08:09.168 --> 00:08:11.013
I am a Waterman, Eric Waterman.

00:08:11.561 --> 00:08:14.327
-Oh, no way.
That's your last name, that's right.

00:08:14.394 --> 00:08:15.966
That's my father here.

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:19.293
My grandmother is beside him.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:21.536
-Could you imagine living anywhere else?

00:08:21.603 --> 00:08:24.531
No, not really. I don't know what I'd do.

00:08:25.163 --> 00:08:27.842
-You're so rooted into this place, huh?
-Yeah.

00:08:28.342 --> 00:08:32.243
I'm the last waterman.
There's no more. I have two daughters.

00:08:32.728 --> 00:08:34.727
That's it. Last man standing.

00:08:37.806 --> 00:08:40.642
This house on the right
is where Buzzy grew up.

00:08:41.156 --> 00:08:43.833
Who we're going to see now.
His real name's Arnold.

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:48.161
They all called him Buzzy 'cause
his younger brother couldn't say brother.

00:08:48.228 --> 00:08:49.533
He'd say buzza.

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:51.661
So they started calling him Buzzy.

00:08:51.728 --> 00:08:53.423
I guess. I might be wrong.

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:55.821
So this is Sprucehead Bridge over there.

00:08:55.888 --> 00:08:57.003
-Oh, okay.

00:08:57.070 --> 00:08:58.018
This is beautiful.

00:08:58.085 --> 00:08:59.893
-This is Buzzy's house on the right.

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:01.533
High school dropout.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:03.108
-That is beautiful.

00:09:03.757 --> 00:09:05.790
Look at his garden. He's doing all that?

00:09:05.857 --> 00:09:09.083
-Yeah. His wife passed away,
and he's just kept her gardens going

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:11.850
to keep her memory alive,
'cause she loved her gardens.

00:09:11.917 --> 00:09:13.432
Look at all these stones here.

00:09:18.892 --> 00:09:20.193
This is beautiful, guys.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:23.062
A true privilege to get in
with some of these old timers.

00:09:23.129 --> 00:09:27.799
You don't just show up and get this,
so we're very lucky to have Eric today.

00:09:27.866 --> 00:09:29.866
[Eric] How you doing, old boy?

00:09:31.405 --> 00:09:34.000
No, I don't have anything to drink
other than water.

00:09:34.067 --> 00:09:36.523
I got water in the truck.
I'll be all right.

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:39.087
[Peter] You want me to call you Buzzy?

00:09:39.154 --> 00:09:40.020
[Buzzy] Hey, you.

00:09:40.087 --> 00:09:42.301
-You want me to call you hey, you.
All right.

00:09:42.368 --> 00:09:45.890
-Anything you want,
as long as I know you're talking to me.

00:09:45.957 --> 00:09:46.786
-All right.

00:09:46.853 --> 00:09:48.550
That's Buzzy back in the day.

00:09:48.617 --> 00:09:51.886
Yeah. It looks like
I'm strangling him, but I'm not.

00:09:51.953 --> 00:09:53.286
[Eric] No, he's not.

00:09:54.127 --> 00:09:56.062
[Peter] How was it
back in the day lobstering?

00:09:56.129 --> 00:09:57.793
A lot better than it is now.

00:09:57.860 --> 00:09:59.918
[Eric] Yeah, for sure.

00:09:59.985 --> 00:10:01.734
I struggle with change.

00:10:01.801 --> 00:10:04.296
I like things the way they were
in the old days.

00:10:04.363 --> 00:10:06.973
It's just like now when we ride to town,

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:08.573
you don't know anybody.

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:11.242
Ten years ago, fifteen years ago,

00:10:11.309 --> 00:10:14.292
you'd be four or five cars
that you would pass in either way

00:10:14.359 --> 00:10:16.928
that you knew the people.
Now they're all strangers.

00:10:16.995 --> 00:10:19.863
You go into Walmart
and you don't hardly know anybody.

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:22.100
In the old days, you just knew everybody.

00:10:22.167 --> 00:10:23.117
-Even out here?

00:10:23.184 --> 00:10:24.457
Oh yes.

00:10:24.524 --> 00:10:26.526
-Guys like Eric
still sticking around though.

00:10:26.593 --> 00:10:28.467
I'm not going to do anything else.

00:10:28.534 --> 00:10:29.934
That's his lifestyle.

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:34.934
That's what he wants to do and this is
what we was all born and brought up with.

00:10:35.001 --> 00:10:37.513
I haven't been able to go now
for seven years.

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:40.780
My wife got colon cancer
and so I had to give up.

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:43.597
She dealt with cancer for

00:10:43.664 --> 00:10:45.915
what, eight years?

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:48.071
-Seven or eight years.
-Gotcha.

00:10:48.138 --> 00:10:51.666
Rather than have
somebody else take care of her.

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:54.053
we kept her right to home.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.114
We don't realize how nice we have it.

00:10:56.181 --> 00:10:57.753
We always want more.

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:02.315
But the little things in life since
I lost her a little over six years ago,

00:11:02.382 --> 00:11:04.882
the little things we take for granted.

00:11:04.949 --> 00:11:05.782
-Like what?

00:11:06.318 --> 00:11:07.211
Hmph.

00:11:07.278 --> 00:11:11.099
Coming home from a day's work
and having your supper already

00:11:11.166 --> 00:11:14.268
setting at the table and eating
with your wife and going to bed

00:11:14.335 --> 00:11:18.454
and doing things in the yard
and it's just totally, totally,

00:11:19.583 --> 00:11:20.416
different.

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:24.978
And she got what she wanted
and she built all of these gardens

00:11:25.045 --> 00:11:28.578
and did she do it to get even
with me for some reason?

00:11:29.046 --> 00:11:33.679
'Cause now I'm trying to take care of it
and I don't know one from another.

00:11:33.746 --> 00:11:36.213
I really don't. Well that's… [scoffs]

00:11:37.016 --> 00:11:39.154
-Dandelions and…
-Oh yeah.

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:41.044
I know tulips.

00:11:41.568 --> 00:11:43.651
And she told me what to do and I did it.

00:11:43.718 --> 00:11:47.303
And that's what I tell everybody now.
I was a moldable male.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.147
There's three ways to do something.

00:11:49.214 --> 00:11:52.547
The right way, the wrong way,
Buzzy's way. The hard way.

00:11:52.614 --> 00:11:54.631
[laughing] The hard way, yeah.

00:11:54.698 --> 00:11:57.243
-You miss being out at sea all the time?

00:11:57.310 --> 00:11:58.756
I'm getting older.

00:11:58.823 --> 00:12:00.378
In a certain sense, yes.

00:12:00.445 --> 00:12:02.827
Because that's all you dealt with

00:12:03.891 --> 00:12:05.558
was what was right there.

00:12:06.165 --> 00:12:06.998
-Yeah.

00:12:07.485 --> 00:12:10.864
Would you guys suggest
someone get into this industry now or now?

00:12:10.931 --> 00:12:12.523
-No.
-No.

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:14.200
Abby, she's fishing but

00:12:14.267 --> 00:12:17.078
I don't think it will ever be
her main source of income.

00:12:17.145 --> 00:12:20.353
She's going to school
and fishing on her own in the summer.

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:22.620
But throw everything on the line.

00:12:23.358 --> 00:12:25.538
[Buzzy] You have to make the decision.

00:12:25.605 --> 00:12:27.611
My wife told me what to do and I did it.

00:12:27.678 --> 00:12:31.793
You put me in my boat and send me
out to haul and I had no trouble.

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:34.293
Mother Nature told me what to do.

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:36.273
-You like the simplicity of it?

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:37.527
Yes. Yeah.

00:12:38.714 --> 00:12:41.248
-Life was pretty straight forward.
-Yes.

00:12:41.315 --> 00:12:44.633
And you know your family needed money.
We needed groceries.

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:48.534
And we didn't get any food stamps
or any federal assistance.

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:52.043
We earned everything
'cause that's the way it was.

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:55.878
Now, you go to the federal government
and they'll give you a coupon.

00:12:55.945 --> 00:12:58.938
You can go into Walmart
and load your grocery cart up

00:12:59.005 --> 00:13:02.005
with anything you want
and they pay for it.

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:05.627
When we was kids and when we was
first married and growing up,

00:13:05.694 --> 00:13:07.813
you needed food, you went and earned it.

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:09.951
-So you're seeing a lot of that in Maine?

00:13:10.018 --> 00:13:11.626
I see it in my own neighborhood.

00:13:11.693 --> 00:13:15.978
But when I'm in the grocery store
and see all these people with their carts

00:13:16.045 --> 00:13:18.489
and they're buying drinking water.

00:13:19.036 --> 00:13:22.303
And I don't.
I drink what comes out of the faucet.

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:25.825
-[Eric] Hard to tell.
-[Buzzy laughs] Yeah.

00:13:25.892 --> 00:13:27.303
All right, Buzzy.

00:13:27.370 --> 00:13:30.176
Nice. That's a firm grip.
You want arm wrestle?

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:32.736
Here you go, Eric.

00:13:32.803 --> 00:13:34.628
Now I'm 85 years old.

00:13:35.254 --> 00:13:37.923
-You count.
-One, two, three.

00:13:37.990 --> 00:13:38.823
Okay.

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:40.949
-Go.
-Come on now.

00:13:41.726 --> 00:13:42.659
[laughs] Nice.

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:48.466
Buzzy locked in.

00:13:49.598 --> 00:13:52.003
I don't want to go too hard, Buzzy.

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:53.857
But that is…

00:13:54.585 --> 00:13:56.102
You got a lot of strength still.

00:13:56.169 --> 00:13:58.236
Take care. Don't crush my hand.

00:13:58.740 --> 00:13:59.573
Why?

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:01.372
Well, you can if you want.

00:14:01.439 --> 00:14:04.239
[Eric] Toxic masculinity.
80 odd years old.

00:14:04.930 --> 00:14:06.730
Yeah, something's happened.

00:14:07.146 --> 00:14:08.247
Yeah.

00:14:08.314 --> 00:14:11.295
Yeah. I'm out on the back porch
with Eric Waterman

00:14:11.362 --> 00:14:13.161
and some guy from Nebraska.

00:14:13.228 --> 00:14:14.068
[Peter laughs]

00:14:14.135 --> 00:14:15.377
Okay. Okay.

00:14:17.208 --> 00:14:18.046
Hey, Buzz.

00:14:19.015 --> 00:14:21.098
Always remember. Never forget.

00:14:21.165 --> 00:14:25.365
There's a sucker born every minute
and two ready to take him on.

00:14:25.432 --> 00:14:26.632
I taught you well.

00:14:36.678 --> 00:14:38.516
-[Abby] Hi.
-[Peter] How you doing?

00:14:38.583 --> 00:14:39.866
-Good. How are you?
-Good.

00:14:39.933 --> 00:14:41.556
-Abby.
-Peter. Nice to meet you.

00:14:41.636 --> 00:14:43.750
I'm having a good time
with your dad today.

00:14:43.817 --> 00:14:46.986
-Picked a good day, didn't he?
-Yeah. Really nice out. [giggles]

00:14:47.053 --> 00:14:49.322
-Abby, how long you been doing this for?

00:14:49.389 --> 00:14:50.858
Since I was like 10.

00:14:50.925 --> 00:14:51.858
-No way.
-Yeah.

00:14:52.544 --> 00:14:54.169
-Do you love it?
-I love it.

00:14:54.236 --> 00:14:55.681
You can't really not.

00:14:55.748 --> 00:14:57.897
-You can't not? What do you love about it?

00:14:57.964 --> 00:14:59.164
That it's outside.

00:15:00.088 --> 00:15:03.821
That I get to do it with family
and close family friends.

00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:06.138
I don't know where this hook is.

00:15:06.205 --> 00:15:08.005
Just drag it with my hands.

00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:20.643
[Peter] How's it been out here?
Good summer?

00:15:20.710 --> 00:15:22.463
[man] We're in pogie season now.

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:25.613
The boats bring in,
get fish in their nets.

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:26.625
-Okay.

00:15:26.692 --> 00:15:28.321
So these guys just caught these?

00:15:28.388 --> 00:15:30.768
-Yeah. These guys hauled
a couple hundred traps.

00:15:30.835 --> 00:15:33.723
-Nice.
What's the value of lobster right now?

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:35.923
-Well, that's all a matter of perspective.

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:39.734
If you're a fisherman,
the price is $5.75 on the slip,

00:15:39.801 --> 00:15:42.887
and they usually get
a little bit extra for good lobsters.

00:15:42.954 --> 00:15:47.043
So roughly $6 a pound in each crate.
We weigh them out to 90 pound crates.

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:49.240
-Okay, and then in a restaurant, how much?

00:15:49.307 --> 00:15:53.242
In a restaurant, a two lobster dinner
could cost you 50 bucks.

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:56.364
-Who's making all the money
in this industry?

00:15:56.431 --> 00:15:58.357
Every set of hands makes a little.

00:15:58.424 --> 00:16:00.223
-Peter?
-Yeah.

00:16:00.290 --> 00:16:01.543
-I watch your stuff.

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:03.722
Oh, thank you, brother. What's your name?

00:16:03.789 --> 00:16:05.368
-My name's Carey.
-Carey.

00:16:05.965 --> 00:16:08.565
-Can I take a look at those guys?
-Sure.

00:16:09.620 --> 00:16:10.453
-Oh, nice.

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:15.157
-They send them up to us as close to
90 pounds as they can get them eyeballing.

00:16:15.224 --> 00:16:17.756
And then we take them
to the scales and weigh them.

00:16:17.822 --> 00:16:19.497
-Okay, and then where do they go?

00:16:19.564 --> 00:16:21.485
We take them off the scales,

00:16:21.552 --> 00:16:23.930
take them to our tank house out back,

00:16:23.997 --> 00:16:26.118
which is basically
a lobster swimming pool.

00:16:26.185 --> 00:16:28.554
Then we put it on a truck
to ship it out from here.

00:16:28.621 --> 00:16:30.461
Where do they end up eventually?

00:16:30.528 --> 00:16:33.443
-Oh, they've been all over the world.
-All over the world?

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:35.739
So why are they moving so fast right now?

00:16:35.806 --> 00:16:36.813
It's just how it is.

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:39.244
For them,
more than anybody, time is money.

00:16:39.311 --> 00:16:42.218
If they screw around
and let the lobsters drain out,

00:16:42.285 --> 00:16:44.358
they're going to lose their water weight.

00:16:44.425 --> 00:16:47.189
Makes out better for the company,
but worse for them.

00:16:47.256 --> 00:16:48.103
-Understood.

00:16:53.610 --> 00:16:55.774
Abby's a lobster fisherman.
She owns a boat.

00:16:55.841 --> 00:16:58.051
I know.
We're getting out with her right now.

00:16:58.118 --> 00:16:59.113
-Wow.

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:01.383
Abby, what do you think
of their technique?

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:04.183
I think that they're both very smart men.

00:17:05.473 --> 00:17:07.243
That's another father and son duo.

00:17:07.310 --> 00:17:09.163
[man] I'm blushing. I'm embarrassed.

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:10.836
I'm going to go home and grieve.

00:17:10.903 --> 00:17:13.263
-That's father and son duo?
-Yeah.

00:17:13.330 --> 00:17:15.383
And then Junior's taking over.

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:17.429
Yep. No, he already did.

00:17:17.941 --> 00:17:19.975
-I have no power.
-[Abby giggles]

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:24.823
[Peter] Lookit, there we go.
Right out to the Atlantic.

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:26.449
So many islands out there.

00:17:26.516 --> 00:17:28.442
So much beauty here in Maine.

00:17:28.509 --> 00:17:32.523
And it's great to see that so many people
are still doing this for work.

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:34.759
And it seems like a thriving industry.

00:17:34.826 --> 00:17:37.152
I believe it goes up the shore
for quite a ways

00:17:37.219 --> 00:17:39.952
where there are fishermen and lobstermen.

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:41.920
And it really is a culture.

00:17:41.987 --> 00:17:44.731
It's really like its own unique thing.

00:17:44.798 --> 00:17:48.623
And very much a multi-generational task.

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:50.493
So, they're setting up the boat.

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:52.013
We're almost ready to go.

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:53.659
Going out to the lobster traps.

00:17:53.726 --> 00:17:56.168
Going out to their island
with their home on it,

00:17:56.235 --> 00:17:58.333
which I'm really excited to see.

00:17:58.400 --> 00:18:00.295
And Eric wanted me to tell you,

00:18:00.362 --> 00:18:04.016
"Hey, let everyone know
that we don't go out fishing this late.

00:18:04.083 --> 00:18:06.898
It's only 'cause, you came up
from Portland this morning.

00:18:06.965 --> 00:18:10.176
Usually we're at the crack of dawn."
So, there you go.

00:18:10.243 --> 00:18:13.863
And also, something I want to tell you
while we're waiting.

00:18:13.930 --> 00:18:15.555
I might be seeing you this week.

00:18:15.622 --> 00:18:19.536
Book tour is happening. If you're
watching this, Day of posting, Saturday.

00:18:19.603 --> 00:18:22.013
Next week, I'm hitting the road

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:25.479
New York, Boston,
Greater Chicago, Greater Detroit,

00:18:26.016 --> 00:18:27.191
Greater Phoenix.

00:18:27.258 --> 00:18:29.725
Hopefully more locations coming soon.

00:18:30.236 --> 00:18:33.403
For book signing,
Your Fellow Americans tour.

00:18:33.470 --> 00:18:35.653
A chance for us to meet in person.

00:18:35.720 --> 00:18:40.114
Scan the code here
on your television or whatever device

00:18:40.181 --> 00:18:42.582
you're watching on or link down below.

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:47.417
That ticket gets you a book,
a signed copy from me.

00:18:47.484 --> 00:18:51.683
Us meeting each other in person.
A handshake, high five, your choice.

00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:56.416
And just a great experience
to finally connect in the flesh and blood.

00:18:56.902 --> 00:18:58.633
And it's happening so soon.

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:00.829
And the weather turned
a bit for the worse,

00:19:00.896 --> 00:19:03.843
but doesn't look like rain.
I think we'll be good.

00:19:03.910 --> 00:19:05.461
Abby, what are you doing there?

00:19:05.528 --> 00:19:07.907
I'm filling the bait bags with pogies.

00:19:07.974 --> 00:19:11.753
So this is what we'll put on the trap
to bait the lobsters.

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:14.636
And putting them in bait bags
makes it last longer.

00:19:14.703 --> 00:19:17.316
So they don't all get eaten
within like a day or two.

00:19:17.383 --> 00:19:19.449
-Okay, so it's harder
to get through the bag?

00:19:19.516 --> 00:19:20.963
-Yeah.
-Gotcha.

00:19:23.500 --> 00:19:25.970
[Eric] See this house
with the little front porch?

00:19:26.037 --> 00:19:27.998
-Yeah.
-That was my great grandfather's.

00:19:28.065 --> 00:19:30.937
-No way.
-And that to the left is his workshop.

00:19:31.004 --> 00:19:33.370
-That's his workshop? That was his house?

00:19:33.437 --> 00:19:36.390
And I asked like 15, maybe 20 years ago,

00:19:36.457 --> 00:19:40.139
the guy who owns this mooring right here,
where my skiff is.

00:19:40.206 --> 00:19:41.448
-That's your skiff?

00:19:41.515 --> 00:19:43.669
-If he ever sold it, I wanted to buy it.

00:19:43.736 --> 00:19:46.222
Because that was where
my great grandfather kept his boat.

00:19:46.289 --> 00:19:47.122
-Wow.

00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:49.377
That's Abby's boat right there.

00:19:49.444 --> 00:19:50.977
-Abby, that's your boat?

00:19:51.044 --> 00:19:52.638
Yep, First Shot.

00:19:52.705 --> 00:19:55.261
-First Shot. So you go out solo mission?

00:19:55.328 --> 00:19:59.145
I take somebody with me,
but yes, I run the boat.

00:19:59.212 --> 00:20:01.290
When did you get your first lobster boat?

00:20:01.357 --> 00:20:04.282
Probably when I was like 12 or 13.

00:20:04.349 --> 00:20:08.139
My dad got me an outboard
with a little hauler on it.

00:20:08.703 --> 00:20:10.711
Like 15 feet long.

00:20:11.544 --> 00:20:14.721
And then that's my next one.
I got that one in 2022.

00:20:14.788 --> 00:20:17.611
-So it's more important
to have a boat than a car, right?

00:20:17.678 --> 00:20:19.968
Yeah. Nicer boat than nicer car.

00:20:21.236 --> 00:20:23.265
[Eric] This is my scallop dragger here.

00:20:23.332 --> 00:20:27.042
So different boats take on
a different functionality for fishing.

00:20:27.109 --> 00:20:29.334
-Yeah.
Different strokes for different folks.

00:20:29.401 --> 00:20:30.433
-There you go.

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:33.909
If I was scallop diving,
I'd go jump off this hard spot right here.

00:20:33.976 --> 00:20:36.603
So it's just gotten better
with the technology?

00:20:36.670 --> 00:20:39.773
Yeah. It's made it a lot easier
for dumb people to do good.

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:42.288
This is a progression of electronics.

00:20:42.762 --> 00:20:45.866
This was popular
in the, I'd say like the 90s.

00:20:46.605 --> 00:20:49.103
And then this is like the next step up.

00:20:49.183 --> 00:20:51.314
-Why do you keep this old stuff?

00:20:51.381 --> 00:20:54.615
Out of respect for the guy that owned
the boat. It's just like part of the boat.

00:20:54.682 --> 00:20:56.905
And I'm just so stubborn. I just held off.

00:20:56.972 --> 00:20:58.916
I was like, I don't need that.
I don't need that.

00:20:58.983 --> 00:21:01.063
-I've been doing it this way.
-Right.

00:21:01.130 --> 00:21:01.963
Kind of like Buzzy.

00:21:02.030 --> 00:21:03.030
[country music]

00:21:18.580 --> 00:21:19.580
[Eric] Hang on.

00:21:19.647 --> 00:21:20.780
[music continues]

00:21:26.319 --> 00:21:27.742
[Eric] Wow. Nothing.

00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:32.820
[Eric] Wow. Worse than I thought.

00:21:33.590 --> 00:21:34.857
[pully wheel whirs]

00:21:36.105 --> 00:21:38.176
[Peter] But they ate all the food, right?

00:21:38.243 --> 00:21:39.163
[Abby] Most of it.

00:21:39.230 --> 00:21:40.097
[Eric] Short.

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:42.943
They're all short.

00:21:43.002 --> 00:21:46.152
-Okay, so if they're not long enough,
you gotta throw them back?

00:21:46.219 --> 00:21:50.352
Yeah. I can tell by looking
that this is probably the only one.

00:21:55.711 --> 00:21:58.841
Short, short, short.
It's a hard time where we are.

00:21:58.908 --> 00:22:01.160
We're just kind of baiting and waiting.

00:22:01.227 --> 00:22:03.208
And these haven't set very long either.

00:22:03.275 --> 00:22:05.531
I hauled them five nights ago.

00:22:13.703 --> 00:22:15.103
There's more there.

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:16.343
Nope.

00:22:17.603 --> 00:22:18.502
Bless it.

00:22:21.039 --> 00:22:23.294
That's a female.
See the eggs under the tail?

00:22:23.361 --> 00:22:25.371
-Oh yeah. So you can't take the females?

00:22:25.438 --> 00:22:28.188
So that's what we do.
See this flipper right here?

00:22:28.255 --> 00:22:30.137
-See how there's no notch in it?
-Yep.

00:22:30.204 --> 00:22:32.312
I'm gonna put a notch in that and that way

00:22:32.379 --> 00:22:35.312
after she drops her eggs,
nobody can keep it.

00:22:36.113 --> 00:22:38.681
-That means it had eggs on it.
-Okay.

00:22:38.748 --> 00:22:40.526
So it protects our industry.

00:22:40.593 --> 00:22:44.098
These are breeders.
This is a small lobster for having eggs.

00:22:44.165 --> 00:22:46.604
-Right.
Look at all those eggs. It's beautiful.

00:22:46.671 --> 00:22:49.204
I'll show you. I'll just go like this.

00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:00.679
[Peter] Okay so this shows
everyone else out here…

00:23:00.746 --> 00:23:03.947
-So even after she drops her eggs
you can't keep it.

00:23:04.014 --> 00:23:06.386
Okay. If I didn't do that,
she dropped her eggs.

00:23:06.453 --> 00:23:09.350
So you flip it over,
there's no eggs but it has a notch,

00:23:09.417 --> 00:23:10.813
you gotta throw it back.

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:12.747
-Everyone plays by those rules?

00:23:12.814 --> 00:23:14.040
Not… I mean…

00:23:15.081 --> 00:23:16.948
Nobody has to notch them but

00:23:18.338 --> 00:23:22.421
people, a lot of people do.
You'll catch a lot of notched lobsters.

00:23:22.488 --> 00:23:24.821
That one's just a little bit under.

00:23:27.160 --> 00:23:28.581
I don't know. [sighs]

00:23:31.766 --> 00:23:36.171
You get claws,
it's like one claw that's we call it junk.

00:23:36.238 --> 00:23:37.705
-Right.
-It's not nice.

00:23:38.368 --> 00:23:41.035
You'll get a run of nice clean lobsters.

00:23:41.538 --> 00:23:42.783
You got these ones with barnacles.

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:46.917
They've been just laying around here.
They're not moving much.

00:23:48.557 --> 00:23:49.423
Yep.

00:23:57.025 --> 00:24:00.043
That's my end marker, that means
that's the last buoy in the string.

00:24:00.110 --> 00:24:02.970
Everyone's gotta put their name
and number on those buoys.

00:24:03.037 --> 00:24:06.484
-So you can come out, you can go
all the way out here and get nothing.

00:24:06.551 --> 00:24:07.418
That happens?

00:24:08.017 --> 00:24:10.011
Not usually, but sometimes.

00:24:10.078 --> 00:24:11.341
You'll get something.

00:24:11.408 --> 00:24:13.290
-So there are days you're losing money.

00:24:13.357 --> 00:24:14.890
Yeah, yeah. Like today.

00:24:16.609 --> 00:24:19.723
It's my day off.
I'm not supposed to make any money.

00:24:21.546 --> 00:24:22.554
Come on, baby.

00:24:24.953 --> 00:24:27.220
Oh, there's gotta be one in there.

00:24:29.530 --> 00:24:31.730
I see one. I see one right there.

00:24:32.215 --> 00:24:33.815
There's one right there.

00:24:34.894 --> 00:24:37.927
See, there's a male.
I can tell by looking at it.

00:24:37.994 --> 00:24:39.590
It indicates it's a male.

00:24:39.657 --> 00:24:41.837
-You sell this to the fish market, right?

00:24:41.904 --> 00:24:42.996
-Yeah.

00:24:43.063 --> 00:24:45.734
-Okay, how much
would that one go for, roughly?

00:24:45.801 --> 00:24:49.265
-Seven bucks probably.
The price is $5.75 a pound

00:24:49.332 --> 00:24:50.233
-Okay.

00:24:50.300 --> 00:24:54.166
And then at a restaurant,
that's like 30 bucks, 40 bucks?

00:24:54.233 --> 00:24:56.589
Yeah. When you eliminate
the middleman, you win.

00:24:56.656 --> 00:24:58.323
But we don't have time and

00:24:59.226 --> 00:25:04.244
we're not set up to 'cause you could
sell them straight to the restaurants,

00:25:04.311 --> 00:25:06.444
we'd make a lot more money, but

00:25:06.972 --> 00:25:09.350
then you gotta have
your own docks to go out of.

00:25:09.417 --> 00:25:10.573
It's a lot of time.

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:13.506
I mean, I sell them
here and there to people.

00:25:13.573 --> 00:25:15.645
There's a campground down the road.

00:25:15.717 --> 00:25:19.017
Put the cooler in the back of my Pontiac
and go for a little cruise

00:25:19.084 --> 00:25:21.774
and sell them lobsters fresh off the boat.

00:25:21.841 --> 00:25:23.544
-Have a drink with them.
-Oh, nice.

00:25:23.611 --> 00:25:25.293
It makes it more enjoyable.

00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:27.130
A little side hustle. You know.

00:25:32.839 --> 00:25:34.376
[Eric] Where we headed, Peter?

00:25:34.443 --> 00:25:35.648
I don't know.

00:25:35.715 --> 00:25:38.058
[in Maine accent]
You can't get there from here.

00:25:38.125 --> 00:25:38.958
[Eric laughs]

00:25:39.613 --> 00:25:41.173
-Good shot.
-Was that good?

00:25:41.240 --> 00:25:42.507
-Yeah, good enough.

00:25:44.052 --> 00:25:45.277
Okay, check this out.

00:25:45.344 --> 00:25:49.813
We're going here at Eben Island
and Eric's last name, Waterman.

00:25:49.880 --> 00:25:52.553
That's how tied in to this place he is.

00:26:01.926 --> 00:26:04.000
So, Eric, this is your family island?

00:26:04.067 --> 00:26:06.002
-Yeah, my sisters and I own it.

00:26:06.521 --> 00:26:09.546
Is that common in Maine?
You own your own island?

00:26:09.613 --> 00:26:12.646
-No, not really.
Not for a local to own an island.

00:26:12.713 --> 00:26:15.243
Out-of-staters, yeah, it's quite common.

00:26:15.310 --> 00:26:16.143
Alright.

00:26:17.224 --> 00:26:19.124
How many guys are out here like you?

00:26:19.191 --> 00:26:21.626
You got your name on the map, basically.

00:26:21.693 --> 00:26:23.454
Six generations.

00:26:23.521 --> 00:26:27.306
There's not as many as there was.
You know, like, in Buzzy's time,

00:26:27.373 --> 00:26:30.464
there was a lot of generational families

00:26:30.531 --> 00:26:32.153
fishing and working together.

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:34.379
There's still a fair amount.

00:26:34.446 --> 00:26:39.246
Most of the harbor, I would say,
is at least second-generation fishermen.

00:26:39.946 --> 00:26:44.103
We're gonna have to paddle this thing
like a canoe getting out of here.

00:26:46.026 --> 00:26:46.966
Land ho!

00:26:49.480 --> 00:26:50.880
Go ahead, Abby, bail.

00:26:53.805 --> 00:26:55.934
Look at that.
There's a wish rock for you.

00:26:56.001 --> 00:26:57.867
-Oh, nice.
-It's got a white band all the way around.

00:26:57.934 --> 00:26:59.467
-Make a wish.
-Good luck?

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:08.544
How often do you guys come out here?

00:27:08.611 --> 00:27:11.356
He stays out here sometimes.

00:27:11.423 --> 00:27:12.823
-No way.
-I just come.

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:17.178
When I get in from haul,
I'll go into the dock, sell my lobsters,

00:27:17.245 --> 00:27:20.273
drop my stern man off,
go to my house, take a shower,

00:27:20.340 --> 00:27:23.607
grab some groceries or whatever
and come back out.

00:27:24.300 --> 00:27:26.660
-And you just totally cut free out here?

00:27:26.727 --> 00:27:30.173
-Yeah, you can walk around naked
if you want, not us, but…

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:31.413
You're the only one out here.

00:27:31.480 --> 00:27:32.547
Permission only.

00:27:33.088 --> 00:27:35.686
-Okay, so this has
been in the family forever.

00:27:35.753 --> 00:27:39.883
My mother had some money
squirreled away for our college.

00:27:40.371 --> 00:27:42.512
I think she bought it in 1979.

00:27:42.579 --> 00:27:43.446
Fairly cheap.

00:27:44.498 --> 00:27:49.313
This little workshop here I built
a couple years ago with a friend of mine.

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:52.223
-That's the shop?
-Yeah, just a little generator.

00:27:52.824 --> 00:27:53.953
-This is a man cave.

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:57.073
-A man cave on your own island is sweet.
-Yeah.

00:27:57.140 --> 00:27:59.990
A hot summer day,
sit here in the shade with the door open

00:28:00.057 --> 00:28:01.924
and just stare at the water.

00:28:02.596 --> 00:28:05.388
Are you sick of lobster at this point
or do you love eating them?

00:28:05.455 --> 00:28:09.099
I usually eat them about twice a year
but I eat as many as I possibly can.

00:28:09.166 --> 00:28:10.398
-Just twice a year?

00:28:10.465 --> 00:28:12.598
Yeah. I like ribeyes. [chuckles]

00:28:13.380 --> 00:28:14.647
Who doesn't, right?

00:28:15.650 --> 00:28:17.185
-And you built this?
-Yeah.

00:28:17.660 --> 00:28:18.493
Nice.

00:28:18.560 --> 00:28:20.286
Friends helped with the shingling.

00:28:20.353 --> 00:28:22.020
Oh, the shag's down there.

00:28:22.863 --> 00:28:24.896
-That island over there, same situation?

00:28:24.963 --> 00:28:29.009
-That's Tommy. These islands both
used to be owned by Ebenezer Thorndike

00:28:29.076 --> 00:28:32.073
and he had a son named Eben
and a son named Thomas

00:28:32.140 --> 00:28:34.523
and he named the islands after his sons.

00:28:34.936 --> 00:28:35.769
-Okay.

00:28:36.250 --> 00:28:38.283
And he also owned Matinic Island

00:28:38.350 --> 00:28:39.993
and Ebenezer,

00:28:40.060 --> 00:28:42.428
he got here because of Henry Knox.

00:28:42.933 --> 00:28:46.023
He was the Secretary of War
for George Washington.

00:28:46.090 --> 00:28:49.120
He settled up here,
they built a great big mansion

00:28:49.187 --> 00:28:52.163
on the river in Thomaston,
St. George River.

00:28:52.750 --> 00:28:55.959
That's where the original was
and they built a replica

00:28:56.026 --> 00:28:57.906
in Thomaston on the hill.

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:00.110
And it's a museum.

00:29:01.420 --> 00:29:03.652
And so they wanted
to get this place settled.

00:29:03.719 --> 00:29:07.783
Get some people up here
and Eben was buddies with Henry Knox

00:29:07.850 --> 00:29:09.350
and he said come on up,

00:29:09.812 --> 00:29:13.143
stake out a chunk of land
and that's what he did.

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:16.088
You got islands,
you got all kinds of land and

00:29:16.155 --> 00:29:19.594
there's a cemetery off Waterman's
Beach Road named Thorndike Cemetery.

00:29:19.661 --> 00:29:21.594
-Okay.
-Like hidden, you know.

00:29:22.160 --> 00:29:24.960
I think it might be
the oldest one in town.

00:29:28.036 --> 00:29:31.025
This is like my carpentry skills.
Can you see?

00:29:31.092 --> 00:29:32.503
-Oh, this is great.

00:29:32.953 --> 00:29:35.703
Just rustic and simple.

00:29:35.770 --> 00:29:39.121
-When I get a good glow going
I fire that speaker up

00:29:39.188 --> 00:29:42.893
and put it on the porch and people text me
song requests it's so loud.

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:45.113
I get texts from that island,

00:29:45.180 --> 00:29:47.004
I get texts from Sprucehead Island,

00:29:47.071 --> 00:29:48.404
from the campground.

00:29:49.128 --> 00:29:51.753
-What are you playing?
-Rock and roll, whatever they want.

00:29:51.820 --> 00:29:52.719
Island DJ.

00:29:53.796 --> 00:29:54.629
It's great.

00:29:55.530 --> 00:29:58.960
This is in case anybody ever gets
blown ashore and gets stranded.

00:29:59.027 --> 00:29:59.860
-Right.

00:29:59.927 --> 00:30:02.584
At least they'll have a good time.
[laughs]

00:30:03.100 --> 00:30:04.167
-What's up here?

00:30:04.761 --> 00:30:07.503
-Check it out.
It's just a little loft with some beds.

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:09.918
[Peter] This is great.

00:30:11.070 --> 00:30:13.013
You got a couple more beds.

00:30:13.080 --> 00:30:15.613
Kite on a fishing pole. That's genius.

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:19.153
-You got your own beach?
-Yeah.

00:30:19.636 --> 00:30:21.360
We'll have lobster out here though.

00:30:21.427 --> 00:30:24.253
We'll cook them on the beach,
clams and lobsters.

00:30:25.417 --> 00:30:26.981
-Water's cold.
-[Abby] Oh, yeah.

00:30:27.048 --> 00:30:28.729
That's like low 50s.

00:30:29.180 --> 00:30:30.933
Yeah, it's low 50s right now.

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:32.964
You can catch lobsters like right here.

00:30:33.031 --> 00:30:34.399
-Just right here?
-Yeah.

00:30:34.840 --> 00:30:36.603
So if I moved up here to Maine

00:30:37.239 --> 00:30:40.386
and I'm like I want to start
lobster fishing is it impossible?

00:30:40.453 --> 00:30:43.753
It would be for you because
you'd be holding the camera in one hand

00:30:43.820 --> 00:30:45.520
and you'd need both hands to fish.

00:30:45.587 --> 00:30:48.001
If I drop the camera
I'm like I'm done with that.

00:30:48.068 --> 00:30:50.279
And I just wanted to fish.
Is there a way in?

00:30:50.346 --> 00:30:52.051
Well there is but it's federal.

00:30:52.118 --> 00:30:55.019
You'd have to fish federal waters
and then you can't bring…

00:30:55.086 --> 00:30:57.836
But in your zone here
I wouldn't be able to do anything.

00:30:57.903 --> 00:30:59.143
No, that's it.

00:31:00.033 --> 00:31:01.663
-That's good for you guys.

00:31:01.730 --> 00:31:05.181
-It seems like they put a limit
on licenses and more people showed up.

00:31:05.248 --> 00:31:06.147
-Okay.

00:31:11.590 --> 00:31:13.419
That's how we lock things in Maine.

00:31:13.486 --> 00:31:15.543
Those are to just keep honest people out.

00:31:15.610 --> 00:31:17.964
You got pretty much no crime
up in these parts?

00:31:18.031 --> 00:31:20.373
Yeah we do. It's getting worse but

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:23.021
it's more in Rockland and more and more

00:31:23.088 --> 00:31:26.153
drug boss people
in their cars bringing drugs in.

00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:28.631
It's like a random road stop

00:31:28.698 --> 00:31:31.413
and they're finding
large amounts of drugs.

00:31:31.480 --> 00:31:33.923
-But up in this town?
-It's pretty good around here.

00:31:33.990 --> 00:31:36.074
They stick out like a sore thumb.

00:31:36.141 --> 00:31:36.974
Right.

00:31:37.830 --> 00:31:40.163
[Eric] Guess we'll paddle on back out.

00:31:44.016 --> 00:31:47.015
This used to be a German lady,
Joe and Hilda.

00:31:47.082 --> 00:31:50.142
I used to work for her after he died
when I was a kid.

00:31:50.209 --> 00:31:51.648
Clearing her land and stuff.

00:31:51.715 --> 00:31:54.927
There used to be a sheep farm.
They had an immaculate sheep farm.

00:31:54.994 --> 00:31:58.017
And then she sold the farm
and they rebuilt.

00:31:58.084 --> 00:31:59.621
They built this thing.

00:31:59.688 --> 00:32:02.123
I think he's
the highest taxpayer in the town.

00:32:02.190 --> 00:32:05.705
And then if you look to the left,
there's another house up in there.

00:32:05.772 --> 00:32:08.219
I think there's three places
on the property.

00:32:08.286 --> 00:32:12.219
You find arrowheads,
Indian artifacts along the shore there.

00:32:12.952 --> 00:32:14.663
Found them on Eben Island.

00:32:14.730 --> 00:32:15.959
Found them up the river.

00:32:16.026 --> 00:32:18.391
-You found them on your island?
Arrowheads? Wow.

00:32:18.458 --> 00:32:21.014
-I found a perfect one out there.
-Who was here?

00:32:21.081 --> 00:32:22.873
-Was it Abenaki?
-Yeah.

00:32:23.479 --> 00:32:25.800
Penawapskewi Indians, they found…

00:32:26.760 --> 00:32:28.740
Spalding Island's right over there.

00:32:28.807 --> 00:32:31.553
-There's a shell pile. You know?
-Yeah.

00:32:31.620 --> 00:32:34.520
The guy was out there,
I don't know 30 years ago.

00:32:34.587 --> 00:32:37.454
He saw a bone
sticking out of the shell pile.

00:32:37.946 --> 00:32:42.863
And he told someone that was into
artifact hunting that knew a lot about it.

00:32:42.930 --> 00:32:46.498
He went out there and excavated it.
It was a skeleton.

00:32:46.565 --> 00:32:49.498
And I think
one of the universities out West,

00:32:50.099 --> 00:32:53.611
they were able to do a DNA test
on it inside one of the bones

00:32:53.678 --> 00:32:56.213
or marrow or whatever was left, something.

00:32:56.735 --> 00:33:00.468
And it came back as part
Native American and part Viking.

00:33:01.400 --> 00:33:02.643
-Oh, interesting.

00:33:02.710 --> 00:33:05.422
And this is where we started.
Right up here somewhere?

00:33:05.489 --> 00:33:06.404
-Yeah. Right here.

00:33:06.471 --> 00:33:08.472
-That's all your family property
back there?

00:33:08.539 --> 00:33:10.165
-This house out front isn't.

00:33:10.232 --> 00:33:12.156
The house in behind is my sister's.

00:33:12.223 --> 00:33:15.247
The house was partially built
with parts from shipwrecks.

00:33:15.314 --> 00:33:16.246
-Oh, no way.

00:33:16.790 --> 00:33:20.469
-Our name went on the chart
because of that name, Thorndike.

00:33:20.536 --> 00:33:23.286
Thorndike Point,
Waterman Point, Waterman's Beach.

00:33:23.353 --> 00:33:27.255
They got the land from Thorndike
when this town first started.

00:33:27.715 --> 00:33:29.858
-You sort of have some rank here,
don't you?

00:33:29.925 --> 00:33:31.781
No, but it doesn't matter to anybody.

00:33:31.848 --> 00:33:34.068
Nobody cares about
your family history or your heritage

00:33:34.135 --> 00:33:35.860
or how long you've been somewhere.

00:33:35.927 --> 00:33:38.207
Everybody's after the almighty dollar.

00:33:38.274 --> 00:33:41.553
And it shows. You can see it in people.
They don't give a sh*t.

00:33:41.620 --> 00:33:45.658
Growing up around this place
and knowing my uncles and my aunts

00:33:45.725 --> 00:33:47.193
and my grandparents,

00:33:47.260 --> 00:33:49.920
if I could go back,
I would have spent a lot more time

00:33:49.987 --> 00:33:51.878
talking to them and learning.

00:33:51.945 --> 00:33:55.370
Closer you get to the source,
the more accurate the reporting is.

00:33:55.437 --> 00:33:57.637
So, by the time it trickles down,

00:33:58.296 --> 00:34:00.162
you can even relate it to the Bible.

00:34:00.229 --> 00:34:03.455
I mean, look how many times
that story's changed. So…

00:34:04.180 --> 00:34:06.543
I hope my kids continue that trend.

00:34:14.070 --> 00:34:16.772
[Peter] How's fishing right now?
What's going on out there, buddy?

00:34:16.839 --> 00:34:18.409
Well, not much really.

00:34:18.476 --> 00:34:20.276
-Really?
-At least we're getting 'em.

00:34:20.343 --> 00:34:22.034
-Is this your sister?
-Yep, that's Nora.

00:34:22.101 --> 00:34:23.572
-You guys fish together?
-Yep.

00:34:23.639 --> 00:34:26.866
Who's better or not better
or worse? It's like you work together.

00:34:26.933 --> 00:34:28.033
-We're even.
-Yes.

00:34:28.100 --> 00:34:29.624
You're out there tomorrow?

00:34:29.691 --> 00:34:31.163
We're going out Monday.

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:32.815
You're getting the boat ready for Monday?

00:34:32.882 --> 00:34:34.021
Yep. Boats.

00:34:34.750 --> 00:34:38.777
We have the Descendant
and we have the old spec.

00:34:39.366 --> 00:34:41.512
Then you got the Nora June,
my grandfather.

00:34:41.579 --> 00:34:43.884
Then you got the Horizon,
my uncle right there.

00:34:43.951 --> 00:34:46.172
-You're the next generation
fishing out here.

00:34:46.239 --> 00:34:49.571
-I think we're like the tenth generation.
-You think you're tenth?!

00:34:49.638 --> 00:34:51.467
We believe so.
We aren't actually sure,

00:34:51.534 --> 00:34:54.693
but we've been fishing
for many, many years.

00:34:54.760 --> 00:34:56.485
-What's your last name?
-McLennan.

00:34:56.552 --> 00:34:59.002
M-C-L-E-N-N-A-N.

00:34:59.069 --> 00:34:59.933
-Okay.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:02.112
What do you think about this year, Nora?

00:35:02.179 --> 00:35:03.554
Been a good year.

00:35:03.615 --> 00:35:05.405
Haven't catched much lobsters, but…

00:35:05.472 --> 00:35:07.039
-We got one today.

00:35:07.106 --> 00:35:07.953
Wow.

00:35:08.020 --> 00:35:09.345
What do you think of that?

00:35:09.412 --> 00:35:10.759
Eh… [chuckles]

00:35:11.625 --> 00:35:13.175
Have you ever seen it that bad?

00:35:13.242 --> 00:35:17.301
Yeah, actually we got four pounds
out of ten traps one day.

00:35:17.368 --> 00:35:19.194
Well, we didn't even get a pound.

00:35:19.261 --> 00:35:20.128
Yeah, that's…

00:35:20.947 --> 00:35:23.913
Yeah, you did worse than us.
That's for sure.

00:35:23.980 --> 00:35:27.647
So we fished worse than
you've ever fished in your life.

00:35:27.727 --> 00:35:29.137
-Yeah, just about.
-Probably.

00:35:29.204 --> 00:35:30.764
-Pretty much.
-In that one haul.

00:35:30.831 --> 00:35:32.073
-Well, truth hurts.

00:35:32.140 --> 00:35:34.839
-But keep it up out there.
-Thank you.

00:35:34.906 --> 00:35:38.519
Without you guys fishing
we're not gonna eat good seafood.

00:35:38.586 --> 00:35:39.433
Right.

00:35:39.500 --> 00:35:42.037
-You're the mother? Thank you for that.
-Yeah.

00:35:42.104 --> 00:35:44.343
That was really cool. They're awesome.

00:35:44.410 --> 00:35:45.811
-Bye.
-See you, guys.

00:35:51.667 --> 00:35:56.179
[Eric] And this, my friend,
is what this town is turning into.

00:35:57.000 --> 00:35:57.833
That.

00:35:58.418 --> 00:36:00.151
-Right.
-Not the prettiest.

00:36:00.745 --> 00:36:03.685
[Eric] Really expensive,
on one acre of land.

00:36:04.665 --> 00:36:06.096
-You're saying four mil?

00:36:06.163 --> 00:36:07.863
-Yeah, it's unreal.

00:36:07.930 --> 00:36:10.303
-And now we're going full circle, Eric.

00:36:10.376 --> 00:36:13.139
-To peace, harmony,
and happiness right here.

00:36:13.206 --> 00:36:14.687
-And you stay here too?

00:36:14.754 --> 00:36:16.502
Yeah, I got a bed in there.

00:36:16.569 --> 00:36:18.373
-Get out!
-I'll let you check it out.

00:36:19.239 --> 00:36:20.211
Maine humor.

00:36:20.278 --> 00:36:21.371
[Peter laughs]

00:36:21.438 --> 00:36:23.473
Okay, Mass… Mass hole.

00:36:23.540 --> 00:36:24.607
Masshole, yeah.

00:36:25.239 --> 00:36:26.727
See? Maintain.

00:36:26.795 --> 00:36:28.905
-Maintain and Mass hole.
-Yeah.

00:36:29.315 --> 00:36:31.238
Don't you have a bunch of Mass friends,
you were saying?

00:36:31.305 --> 00:36:35.822
Yeah, I gave a couple to my buddies,
they're on Wicked Tuna.

00:36:35.889 --> 00:36:38.391
They thought they were funnier than hell.

00:36:38.903 --> 00:36:41.703
-Okay, so Eric…
-They have a certain type of personality.

00:36:41.770 --> 00:36:44.503
I don't give those
to everybody from Mass.

00:36:45.130 --> 00:36:46.189
[Peter laughs]

00:36:46.256 --> 00:36:48.913
Okay,
so you're saying here's the friction,

00:36:48.980 --> 00:36:51.395
big money coming in from other places.

00:36:51.462 --> 00:36:53.873
Yeah, well this is a perfect example.

00:36:53.940 --> 00:36:56.175
I get along with those people great.

00:36:56.655 --> 00:36:58.573
I don't… But this isn't…

00:36:58.640 --> 00:37:00.703
I've been here my whole life so far.

00:37:00.770 --> 00:37:02.320
This is just what's happening to me.

00:37:02.387 --> 00:37:05.633
It's like Vermont and it's probably
happening in New Hampshire.

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:09.567
They buy a second home
and they turn it into the Taj Mahal, so…

00:37:10.151 --> 00:37:11.263
It doesn't bother me.

00:37:11.330 --> 00:37:13.309
I get more joy out of this
than they do out of that.

00:37:13.376 --> 00:37:15.769
-This is your Taj?
-This is my Taj Mahal.

00:37:15.836 --> 00:37:17.003
-Let's check it out.

00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:19.246
Oh, look at that. That's the view.

00:37:19.710 --> 00:37:21.045
What would it take?

00:37:21.112 --> 00:37:23.367
Everyone's got a price
with their real estate.

00:37:23.434 --> 00:37:26.919
If your neighbors gave you six mil,
you're gonna leave, aren't you?

00:37:26.986 --> 00:37:27.912
-No.
-Come on.

00:37:28.590 --> 00:37:30.120
Six mil is not much money.

00:37:30.187 --> 00:37:33.093
I could blow through that
in two years in Vegas. No.

00:37:33.546 --> 00:37:35.999
-What would be the price
to sell this shack?

00:37:36.066 --> 00:37:38.188
Lifetime supply of White Claw. No.

00:37:38.255 --> 00:37:39.424
[laughter]

00:37:39.491 --> 00:37:40.813
I don't have one.

00:37:40.880 --> 00:37:44.506
-You wouldn't no matter what?
You're going to stand firm on principles?

00:37:44.573 --> 00:37:47.492
And hold out. You thinking about
buying some land in Maine?

00:37:47.559 --> 00:37:50.646
I'm not. I just test people
on this question all the time.

00:37:50.713 --> 00:37:53.576
-I'm too stubborn.
-I was just in the Arkansas Delta.

00:37:53.643 --> 00:37:56.593
And they're putting solar panels
up over the farmer's fields

00:37:56.660 --> 00:37:58.220
and the farmer wasn't happy about it.

00:37:58.287 --> 00:38:00.923
And I'm like, there's a price
that you'd sell, wouldn't you?

00:38:00.990 --> 00:38:03.165
And he's like, nah, nah.
And then I pushed on.

00:38:03.232 --> 00:38:05.702
He's like, yeah, five mil,

00:38:06.534 --> 00:38:07.383
you know…

00:38:07.450 --> 00:38:08.831
But you're saying no, Eric.

00:38:08.898 --> 00:38:12.271
If they were willing to pay
five million or six million for this,

00:38:12.338 --> 00:38:14.317
I'd sell them some land somewhere else.

00:38:14.384 --> 00:38:15.223
Not this.

00:38:15.290 --> 00:38:18.357
There's not many
generational Mainers that can

00:38:18.424 --> 00:38:20.544
look at the ocean
from their camp or house.

00:38:20.611 --> 00:38:24.399
I could build a house here if I want.
I have a house right up the road.

00:38:24.466 --> 00:38:28.780
I'm less than a mile from my house
and it's like being in a whole nother…

00:38:28.847 --> 00:38:30.833
You know, a whole nother place.

00:38:30.900 --> 00:38:32.169
I could walk to my house.

00:38:32.236 --> 00:38:35.553
I could ride a four-Wheeler
through the woods to my house.

00:38:35.620 --> 00:38:36.462
So…

00:38:36.529 --> 00:38:39.988
-So people might think it was all…
might sound like a douche right now

00:38:40.055 --> 00:38:42.904
but people might say
that it was all given to you, right?

00:38:42.971 --> 00:38:44.260
You didn't have to work for it.

00:38:44.327 --> 00:38:48.299
Yeah, I haven't worked for anything.
It's all just fallen in my lap.

00:38:48.845 --> 00:38:50.771
I never met a banker I liked.

00:38:50.838 --> 00:38:52.638
But I've met a lot of them. [laughs]

00:38:54.485 --> 00:38:56.099
No, I've had some help.

00:38:57.260 --> 00:38:59.173
But I had to prove myself.

00:38:59.240 --> 00:39:02.040
I'm very fortunate.
I'm lucky. I'm blessed.

00:39:02.840 --> 00:39:04.404
And… I'm proud.

00:39:05.550 --> 00:39:08.866
You just gotta put your head down
and keep on going.

00:39:08.933 --> 00:39:12.503
You got your ups and downs.
It's just like the stock market.

00:39:12.570 --> 00:39:16.237
But you find the level
and just hold there and maintain.

00:39:17.490 --> 00:39:20.793
Today's catch, we'll call it a down
but it was a good experience.

00:39:20.860 --> 00:39:23.151
Yeah, we just went for the experience.

00:39:23.218 --> 00:39:27.651
If you really want to see some action
you gotta come up in probably like

00:39:27.717 --> 00:39:29.397
three and a half weeks from now.

00:39:29.464 --> 00:39:31.462
-Okay. Can we go in?
-Yeah.

00:39:32.410 --> 00:39:34.023
Another Maine lock, see?

00:39:34.090 --> 00:39:35.266
No one can get in.

00:39:35.333 --> 00:39:37.629
-It's locked.
-[Peter chuckles]

00:39:39.700 --> 00:39:41.833
And this is the… You sleep down here?

00:39:41.900 --> 00:39:44.016
-Yes!
-This is great!

00:39:44.083 --> 00:39:46.533
It's just everything you need.
Simple as it comes.

00:39:46.600 --> 00:39:50.753
It's awesome. I got a guitar there.
I could embarrass myself playing that.

00:39:50.820 --> 00:39:54.120
-There's a couple photos I took.
-"You suck less than most people."

00:39:54.187 --> 00:39:56.987
Yeah, that's good friends
giving me that for my birthday.

00:39:57.054 --> 00:39:59.323
I thought it was great
so I put it up there.

00:39:59.390 --> 00:40:01.721
That's Joe DiMaggio's nephew.

00:40:01.788 --> 00:40:04.321
A good friend of mine. He passed away.

00:40:05.000 --> 00:40:07.732
His father was Dom DiMaggio.
He played for the Yankees.

00:40:07.799 --> 00:40:11.273
He had a house on Sprucehead Island
and he built a log cabin

00:40:11.340 --> 00:40:13.673
on Waterman's Beach Road down here.

00:40:14.326 --> 00:40:18.183
And that's the Constitution.
I built that in high school.

00:40:18.250 --> 00:40:20.953
It's just everything's miniature.
Look at this thing.

00:40:21.020 --> 00:40:23.344
A little bigger than my hand,
but it's perfect.

00:40:23.411 --> 00:40:25.871
It's all you need.
It's old school tiny house.

00:40:25.938 --> 00:40:29.738
-This is so cool.
This is an old school tiny house, right?

00:40:30.190 --> 00:40:32.793
Alright, Eric.
Anything else you want to say?

00:40:32.860 --> 00:40:34.068
Always be good to people

00:40:34.135 --> 00:40:37.970
because you don't know what
anyone's been through except yourself.

00:40:38.037 --> 00:40:39.093
-That's true.

00:40:39.170 --> 00:40:41.479
If anyone wants to get one
of your fine hats here…

00:40:41.546 --> 00:40:45.299
Are you selling
the limited edition Massachusetts hat?

00:40:45.366 --> 00:40:46.925
Might be too offensive to some.

00:40:46.992 --> 00:40:48.555
I know how sensitive people are

00:40:48.622 --> 00:40:51.840
and there's a lot of Karens and
thin-skinned people in the world.

00:40:51.907 --> 00:40:55.087
For those that are,
they can buy the hat from you. Okay.

00:40:55.154 --> 00:40:57.289
Ask for the vulgar edition of…

00:40:57.356 --> 00:41:00.579
Okay. Or if they want to go
a little lighter on the…

00:41:00.646 --> 00:41:02.777
-All different colors.
-Okay. Cool.

00:41:02.844 --> 00:41:05.275
Pick the colors.
Mix and match. Maintain.

00:41:05.710 --> 00:41:09.449
And then, all of you,
hope to see you next week.

00:41:09.516 --> 00:41:10.533
Which is crazy.

00:41:10.600 --> 00:41:12.015
I'm doing a book tour, Eric.

00:41:12.082 --> 00:41:14.143
-Yeah?
-Yeah. I'm going all over the country.

00:41:14.210 --> 00:41:16.918
For the first time,
I'm not gonna be behind the screen.

00:41:16.985 --> 00:41:19.492
-I'm actually meeting people in person.
-Yeah.

00:41:19.559 --> 00:41:22.578
You should practice shaking hands
like with Buzzy. I bet you…

00:41:22.645 --> 00:41:25.413
Buzzy had a strong shake.
Nice shake, Eric.

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:28.512
-Thanks for today. Appreciate it, bro.
-You're welcome.

00:41:28.579 --> 00:41:31.181
And this is part of
a greater Maine series.

00:41:31.773 --> 00:41:33.723
Portland, Lewiston,

00:41:34.415 --> 00:41:37.263
a mill town
that I'm going to in a couple days,

00:41:37.815 --> 00:41:39.247
and then far north.

00:41:39.314 --> 00:41:42.242
So, five different Maines,
five different stories.

00:41:42.309 --> 00:41:44.657
-Thanks, Eric.
-You're welcome. Come again.

00:41:44.724 --> 00:41:47.443
Thanks for coming along, guys.
Until the next one.

00:41:47.510 --> 00:41:48.510
[country music]

